course: dictionary
lesson: 13687
title: Inmate: a person kept inside
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An inmate is a person who is kept in a place and is not free to leave. Most of the time, this means a prisoner. «The inmate counted the days until he could go home.» Counting days tells you he is locked up, not free, so inmate means a prisoner.
explain: Look at the words around the word. «Each inmate had to be back in their cell by eight o'clock.» The words cell and eight o'clock are clues: a cell is a small room in a prison, so an inmate must be someone who lives there because they are locked up.
explain: Some words come in phrases that help too. A prison inmate is a person kept in a prison. A fellow inmate is another person kept in the same place, so the word fellow means "another one, sharing the same situation." «Tom's fellow inmate taught him to play chess.»
explain: In very old books, an inmate could also mean a person living in a hospital or another institution. You will mostly meet the prison meaning in news and stories, so when you are not sure, check the words around it and ask: is this person locked up?
fact: An inmate is a person kept in a prison.
fact: Inmate is a noun, and its plural is inmates.
fact: A prison inmate is someone kept in a prison; a fellow inmate is another person kept in the same place.
fact: Long ago, inmate could also mean someone living in a hospital or other institution.
fact: To check a meaning, swap it into the sentence: if it still makes sense when you say "prisoner", the meaning is right.
exercise: «The inmate looked out of the small window at the grey sky.» What is an inmate? | A teacher in a school | A person who works as a prison guard | *A person who is kept in a prison | A visitor who comes to see a friend for lunch
exercise: «Each inmate had to be back in their cell by eight o'clock.» What does this tell the reader? | The inmates are free to go to the shops | The inmates are having a party | The inmates are going on a holiday | *The inmates are locked up and not free to leave
exercise: «Tom's fellow inmate taught him to play chess.» What does fellow inmate mean? | A teacher at the school | A friend who lives in the next street | A person in charge of the prison | *Another person kept in the same prison
test: «The inmate was given a new bed and a meal.» In this sentence, who is the inmate? | A visitor who has come to stay for a week | A person who cooks the meals | A person who is kept in a prison | *A person kept in a prison, so they are not free to leave
test: Which sentence uses inmate in its usual, everyday meaning? | Mum is an inmate of the local library | The inmate went to school on the bus | *The inmate was taken to her cell after the trial | The inmate of the town is very friendly
exam: «The news said the inmate had escaped from the prison.» Which word tells you the inmate was locked up before the escape? | *prison | said | escaped | news
